{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2820 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "489d59538091601f45e59468e028d83962a05c796fc91c8c9ee6eab41e5192e3" :edb/db-before-t 2819 :edb/db-after-t 2820 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536132 50 #inst "2026-10-09T16:08:13.819Z" 13194139536132 true] [17592186151874 1100 "Find a definite integral with respect to $\\theta$ that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} (y^2-x) \\, \\textrm ds$, where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r = \\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\le \\theta \\le\\dfrac{\\pi}4$." 13194139536132 false] [17592186151874 1100 "Find a definite integral with respect to $\\theta$ that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} (y^2-x) \\, \\textrm ds,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r = \\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\le \\theta \\le\\dfrac{ \\pi}4.$" 13194139536132 true] [17592186151874 1145 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos^2\\theta$ and $y=\\cos\\theta \\sin{\\theta}$, where $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}4.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) =y^2-x$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{ }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta (-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536132 true] [17592186151874 1145 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,\n$$\n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ \\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} θ, \\\\\ny &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ \\cdot \\sin θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\cos^2\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\cos\\theta \\sin{\\theta}\n$$\n\n, where\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}4\n$$\n\nNow, if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =y^2-x\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ) &= (\\cos θ\\sin θ)^{2} - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} θ\\sin^{2} θ - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} θ(\\sin^{2} θ - 1) \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} θ(-\\cos^{2} θ) \\\\\n&=-\\cos^{4} θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dr}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(\\cos θ) =-\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ &= \\sqrt{(\\cos θ)^{2} + (-\\sin θ)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)(y^{2} - x)ds &= ∫_{0}^{π/4}f(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ)\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/4} - \\cos^{4} θ \\cdot 1 \\cdot dθ \\\\\n&= - ∫_{0}^{π/4}\\cos^{4} θdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536132 false] [17592186151875 1100 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac\\pi 2$." 13194139536132 false] [17592186151875 1100 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac\\pi 2.$" 13194139536132 true] [17592186151875 1145 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos\\theta$ and $y=\\sin\\theta$, where $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}2.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-(0-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536132 true] [17592186151875 1145 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,\n$$\n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ = \\cos θ, \\\\\ny &= r\\sin θ = \\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\n, where\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ) &= \\frac{\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ}{\\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= \\csc^{2} θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dr}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(1) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ = \\sqrt{1^{2} + 0^{2}} = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)\\frac{x^{2} + y^{2}}{y^{2}}ds &= ∫_{π/6}^{π/2}f(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ)\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/6}^{π/2}\\csc^{2} θ \\cdot 1 \\cdot dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/6}^{π/2}\\csc^{2} θdθ \\\\\n&=-\\cot θ \\mid _{π/6}^{π/2} \\\\\n&= - (0 - \\sqrt{3}) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536132 false] [17592186151876 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C} xy(1-x^2) \\,\\textrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the polar curve $r=\\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$." 13194139536132 false] [17592186151876 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C} xy(1-x^2) \\,\\textrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the polar curve $r=\\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" 13194139536132 true] [17592186151876 1145 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos^2\\theta$ and $y=\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta$, where $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) = xy(1-x^2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\cos\\theta,$ $\\textrm d u=-\\sin\\theta\\,\\textrm d \\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}-{u}^{3}(1-{u}^{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{3}-{u}^{7})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{u}^{8}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{8})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536132 true] [17592186151876 1145 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,\n$$\n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ \\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} θ, \\\\\ny &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ \\cdot \\sin θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\cos^2\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\n, where\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = xy(1-x^2)\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ) &= \\cos^{2} θ \\cdot \\cos θ\\sin θ(1 - (\\cos^{2} θ)^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dr}{dθ} = \\frac{d}{dθ}(\\cos θ) =-\\sin θ\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ &= \\sqrt{(\\cos θ)^{2} + (-\\sin θ)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)xy(1 - x^{2})ds &= ∫_{0}^{π/2}f(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ)\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ) \\cdot 1 \\cdot dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ)dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u=-\\sin\\theta\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ)dθ &= ∫_{1}^{0} - u^{3}(1 - u^{4})du \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(u^{3} - u^{7})du \\\\\n&= [\\frac{u^{4}}{4} - \\frac{u^{8}}{8}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{8}) - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536132 false] [17592186152083 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385163 13194139536132 true] [17592186152083 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385170 13194139536132 true] [17592186152083 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385177 13194139536132 true] [17592186152084 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385184 1319413953613