ere $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4({cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4([\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]-[-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536132 true] [17592186152661 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536132 true] [17592186152661 1100 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" 13194139536132 true] [17592186152661 1100 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $∫_(C)(x^{2} + y^{2})ds$, where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r = 2\\sin θ$ for $0 \\le θ \\le π$?" 13194139536132 false] [17592186152661 1145 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\the